---
// Page shell for /personal-finance/retirement-accounts: intro + the
// RetirementAccountsModule island (Social Security & Roth), US-specific.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import RetirementAccountsModule from "../../components/RetirementAccountsModule.tsx";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import RegionNote from "../../components/RegionNote.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Retirement Accounts: Social Security & Roth"
  description="The two big US retirement-account decisions: when to claim Social Security (the guaranteed, inflation-adjusted income you can't outlive) and whether to save in a Roth or Traditional account — starting with the employer match you should never leave behind."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Personal Finance · Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Your retirement accounts</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Two decisions shape how much of your retirement you actually keep. When you
      claim Social Security sets a guaranteed, inflation-adjusted income for life —
      and waiting means bigger checks. Roth versus Traditional comes down to your tax
      rate now versus later. Both are worth getting right; neither needs a login.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <RegionNote>
      US-focused — these tools model US retirement accounts: Social Security claiming
      ages and survivor benefits, and Roth vs. Traditional IRAs / 401(k)s with the
      US split between ordinary-income and later tax rates. The trade-offs
      (delayed guaranteed income; paying tax now vs. later) exist elsewhere, but the
      rules and account names here are American. This is educational only, not
      personalized advice — big claiming and conversion decisions are worth checking
      with a professional.
    </RegionNote>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <RetirementAccountsModule client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The two decisions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>When to claim Social Security.</strong> Claiming later means bigger
          checks for the rest of your life — inflation-adjusted longevity insurance
          you can't outlive. The breakeven age and a spouse's survivor benefit decide
          whether waiting pays for your situation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Roth or Traditional.</strong> Roth is taxed now, Traditional is
          taxed later, so it turns on whether your tax rate is higher today or in
          retirement. And whatever you choose, capture the full employer match first:
          an instant, guaranteed return nothing else matches.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These are the account decisions behind a retirement plan; for whether the pile
        actually lasts once you're spending it, see the
        <a href="/tools/retirement">Will Your Money Last? tool</a>. Educational only,
        not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="retirement-accounts" />

  <section class="container pf-related">
    <h2 class="pf-related-heading">Keep exploring</h2>
    <ul class="grid grid--2 pf-related-grid">
      <li>
        <a href="/tools/retirement" class="card">
          <h3>Will your money last?</h3>
          <p>Stress-test your spending against real market history and see sequence-of-returns risk — the drawdown half of retirement.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/personal-finance/next-dollar" class="card">
          <h3>Fund it in the right order</h3>
          <p>Match, debt, Roth, or brokerage? See the one next step in the priority ladder for every extra dollar.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
  .pf-related { margin-top: var(--space-xl); }
  .pf-related-heading { font-size: var(--step-1); padding-bottom: var(--space-xs); border-bottom: var(--border); margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
  .pf-related-grid { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .pf-related-grid a.card { height: 100%; text-decoration: none; color: inherit; display: block; }
  .pf-related-grid h3 { margin: 0 0 0.3rem; font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .pf-related-grid p { margin: 0; color: var(--color-text-soft); font-size: var(--step--1); }
</style>
